</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Contract Theory for Provision</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contract theory is a branch of economics that examines how individuals and organizations design contracts under conditions of information asymmetry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guruganesh et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Due to its effectiveness, contract theory has been widely applied in wireless networks to ensure high-quality and reliable provision, including content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ismail et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, services <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, and resources <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For instance, the authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> proposed a contract-theoretic model under Prospect Theory (PT) to motivate high-quality sensing data sharing in healthcare metaverses. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, the authors applied contract theory to optimize payment scheme design of clients, thereby ensuring the provision of high-quality AI-generated content services. In addition, in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, the authors proposed a multi-dimensional contract-theoretic model under PT to incentivize roadside units to contribute bandwidth and computing resources, thereby enabling the seamless migration of embodied agent AI twins. However, the aforementioned works fail to simultaneously address the adverse selection and moral hazard problems arising from information asymmetry. Most importantly, <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">how to design a contract-theoretic model for high-quality content provision remains an open problem, particularly in the context of Web 3.0, where research on this aspect is still lacking.</span> For clarity, Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2.1. Web 3.0 and UGC ‣ 2. Related Work ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the comparison of the current works and our work in contract design under asymmetric information.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3. </span>DRL for Contract Design</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DRL is an AI technique that combines deep learning and RL, enabling agents to learn decision-making policies by interacting with environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haarnoja et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Since traditional contract design methods, such as heuristic optimization algorithms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, often struggle in practical environments where complete information is unavailable, recent studies have explored the use of DRL algorithms to design optimal contracts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, the authors adopted PPO algorithms to determine optimal contracts. Moreover, GDMs possess strong generative capabilities that make them well-suited for dynamic network optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Therefore, some researchers have developed diffusion-based DRL algorithms for optimal contract design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For instance, the authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> employed a diffusion-based SAC algorithm to generate optimal contracts under PT. Building on this, the authors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> proposed a sustainable diffusion-based SAC algorithm that incorporates dynamic structured pruning techniques to reduce the number of parameters in diffusion-based actor networks, thereby identifying optimal feasible contracts. However, GDMs still face challenges such as high computational costs, difficulties in debugging, and inconsistent performance in low-dimensional environments. Motivated by the successful integration of MoE and PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, in this paper, we propose an improved MoE-based PPO algorithm for optimal contract design.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first introduce the lifecycle of UGC in Web 3.0 and highlight one of its potential risks. We then propose an LMM-based contract-theoretic model to address this risk.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04765v1/UGC.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/119;" width="452" height="119" alt="A flow diagram showing the UGC process.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1. </span>The lifecycle of UGC in Web 3.0, mainly consisting of five steps: identity authentication, content creation, decentralized storage, content verification, and economic incentives.<span id="acmlabel1" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A flow diagram showing the UGC process.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>UGC Lifecycle</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Web 3.0, users have control over their own generated content, without any single entity monopolizing the network infrastructure or user information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. As shown in Fig. <a href="#acmlabel1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the lifecycle of UGC in Web 3.0 is presented as follows:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1. Identity Authentication:</span> In Web 3.0, users first generate cryptographic key pairs and establish decentralized identities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. The key pairs consist of public and private keys, where the public key functions as an on-chain identifier and the private key enables signing and authorization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Additionally, users can leverage privacy-preserving cryptographic methods, such as zero-knowledge proofs, to establish verifiable claims without the need for centralized intermediaries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2. Content Creation:</span> Users can liberally generate content with the assistance of AI tools or entirely through autonomous AI agents under predefined parameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Once creation is completed, the content is hashed, and the hash is digitally signed using the private key of users, binding the content to their decentralized identities and creating an immutable proof of authorship and integrity.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 3. Decentralized Storage:</span> The UGC is uploaded to decentralized storage platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, while metadata, including the hash and authorship details, is registered on the blockchain for performance and traceability. Moreover, users disseminate their generated content through peer-to-peer networks for easy retrieval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 4. Content Verification:</span> Users in the peer-to-peer network can verify content authenticity by checking the signature against the public key of creators and matching the stored hash. During verification, smart contracts can automatically manage authorization and execute rules, such as access control, following the pre-set terms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 5. Economic Incentives:</span> In Web 3.0 ecosystems, tokenized incentive platforms (e.g., DAOs) can directly reward users for their contributions to generating content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, either by distributing tokens from community treasuries or through smart contracts that encode predefined incentive rules <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The primary purpose of economic incentive design is to align user participation with value creation. However, tokenized incentive platforms may lack awareness of the current effort level of users, resulting in a situation of information asymmetry. Some self-interested users would deliberately generate low-quality content to serve their interests, disrupting the global interests of the Web 3.0 ecosystem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. Therefore, it is necessary to design a reliable incentive mechanism to curb this phenomenon, thereby ensuring the sustainable operation of Web 3.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contract theory is an effective economic tool to address the issue of information asymmetry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, which focuses on designing contracts that motivate agents to act in ways that benefit both sides, even when they have different goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Adverse selection</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moral hazard</span> are two fundamental information asymmetries in contract theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guruganesh et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Specifically, adverse selection refers to a situation in which agents, possessing private information unknown to the principal, tend to select biased contracts to obtain more benefits at the expense of the principal. To mitigate this issue, the principal must design incentive-compatible contracts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guruganesh et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Moral hazard arises after contract selection, when the principal may find it difficult to directly supervise agent actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guruganesh et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Although the principal can employ metrics to indirectly evaluate performance, agents may still resort to opportunistic behaviors that deviate from the interest of the principal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most of the current research focuses on addressing one of the information asymmetries, either adverse selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> or moral hazard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ismail et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. To simultaneously address these two forms of information asymmetries, researchers employ physical equations to quantify agent effort and incorporate them into the utility function of agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Doe et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However, this way still cannot objectively and exactly quantify the real effort of agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Since UGC in Web 3.0 is inherently multimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and LMMs demonstrate strong capabilities in analyzing multimodal data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we develop and employ LMM agents to quantify the generation effort of users and propose an LMM-based contract-theoretic model to promote high-quality UGC in Web 3.0. In the following, we formulate the utilities of the reward platform and its users, respectively.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span>User utility</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The user utility depends on the received rewards from the platform and the cost of generating content. In particular, the cost of generating content depends on the quality of content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, that is, the higher the quality, the greater the generation cost. Moreover, the quality of UGC is generally associated with user reputation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. Once users multiply issue low-quality content, their on-chain reputation values will be decreased. Thus, we denote the quality of UGC as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> and utilize it to approximate the cost <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]</annotation></semantics></math> represents user reputation. The utility of users can be expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{U}=f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{U}=f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the received rewards associated with the user reputation, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> is a pre-defined weight parameter, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is the unit cost of generating content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2. </span>Reward platform utility</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The utility of the reward platform depends on the quality of UGC and the rewards given to users. Specifically, high-quality content will attract more users to consume tokens, thereby enhancing the value and liquidity of the platform token <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Therefore, the utility of the reward platform toward each user is expressed as</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{P}=\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{P}=\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is the coefficient utilized to fine-tune the utility of the reward platform <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> represents the threshold of UGC quality.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04765v1/LMMContract.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:390/472;" width="390" height="472" alt="A procedure of UGC evaluation through LMM agents.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2. </span>A procedure of UGC evaluation through an LMM agent. <span id="S3.F2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Part A</span> illustrates the initial setting of the LMM agent. <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Part B</span> introduces few-shot prompting to enhance its performance of UGC evaluation. <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Part C</span> presents evaluation cases conducted by the LMM agent, where CoT prompting is employed to guide it to generate accurate results. Note that in practical implementation, we append the prompt <span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“Please directly output the quality rating”</span> to accelerate the UGC evaluation.<span id="acmlabel2" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">A procedure of UGC evaluation through LMM agents.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.3. </span>LMM-based contract formulation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In practice, the platform can observe only the past on-chain reputation values of users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, but cannot precisely determine their current effort levels and reputation status, indicating that there exists an information asymmetry between the platform and users. Therefore, we define the user reputation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> as the user type, which is a continuous variable. In the LMM-based contract-theoretic model, the platform, acting as the principal, is responsible for designing a contract consisting of a series of items, denoted by <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega=\{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega=\{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each user in the platform selects the appropriate contract item according to its type before content generation, which can mitigate the problem of adverse selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to employing multiple indicators to indirectly calculate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, the platform leverages trained LMM agents to directly evaluate the quality of UGC and obtain a precise value of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. As shown in Fig. <a href="#acmlabel2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2.2. Reward platform utility ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we present a procedure of UGC evaluation through an LMM agent (i.e., a GPT-5 agent). Specifically, we utilize the LMM agent to evaluate the quality of images generated by Stable Diffusion<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              Stable Diffusion is available at <a href="https://huggingface.co/spaces/stabilityai/stable-diffusion" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/spaces/stabilityai/stable-diffusion</a></span></span></span>, which mainly considers two factors: clarity and aesthetics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Similarly, we can employ LMM agents to evaluate the quality of other multimodal content, such as text, by incorporating the corresponding factors. To enhance the performance of the LMM agent, we first apply few-shot prompting by providing a small set of evaluation examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brown et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, which enhances its adaptability and reasoning capability. After this initialization, we utilize CoT prompting to guide the LMM agent to conduct step-by-step evaluation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Through prompt engineering, the LMM agent is thereby enabled to generate more accurate evaluation results <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Guided by LMM agent feedback, the platform can monitor user effort in content generation, thereby alleviating the problem of moral hazard. To guarantee that each user automatically chooses the contract item designed for its specific type, the feasible contract should satisfy the following <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Individual Rationality (IR)</span> and <span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Incentive Compatibility (IC)</span> constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IR Constraints:<span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Users obtain a non-negative utility when they select the contract item corresponding to their types, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{U}=f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{U}=f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Definition 0.</h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">IC Constraints:<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Users achieve the maximum utility by selecting the contract item designed for their types rather than any other item, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(4)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi}),\&gt;\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.607em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi}),\&gt;\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">According to the above IR and IC constraints, we formulate the problem of maximizing the expected utility of the reward platform as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 1:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Problem 1:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 1:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))}\int_{\underline{\phi}}^{\overline{\phi}}[\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi)]\mathcal{F}(\phi)\,\mathrm{d}\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))}\int_{\underline{\phi}}^{\overline{\phi}}[\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi)]\mathcal{F}(\phi)\,\mathrm{d}\phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E5Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.440em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E5Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E5Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq I,\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq I,\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability density function of user reputation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>. According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deshprabhu, 2024</a>)</cite>, node reputation in distributed networks follows the beta distribution. Thus, we model user reputation <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> as a beta-distributed random variable, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(6)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}(\phi)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}\phi^{\alpha-1}(1-\phi)^{\beta-1},\&gt;\alpha,\beta&gt;0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}(\phi)=\frac{\Gamma(\alpha+\beta)}{\Gamma(\alpha)\Gamma(\beta)}\phi^{\alpha-1}(1-\phi)^{\beta-1},\&gt;\alpha,\beta&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the gamma function, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are the shape parameters of the distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, it is challenging to directly solve Problem 1 under the existing IR (<a href="#S3.E3" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and IC (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) constraints. A common approach is to reduce the number of IR and IC constraints to decrease the solution complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. Thus, we first reduce the number of IR constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">According to the IC constraints, we have</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(7)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.2" class="ltx_p">If the IR constraint of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> is satisfied, the IR constraints for all the other values of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> will automatically hold. Thus, the IR constraints can be simplified as</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(8)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">To reduce the number of IC constraints, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> is required to satisfy the monotonicity condition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>)</cite>. However, users with high reputation values may also unintentionally generate low-quality content. Therefore, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> does not strictly satisfy the monotonicity condition. Based on the above analysis, Problem 1 can be simplified as</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(9)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 2:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Problem 2:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 2:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))}\int_{\underline{\phi}}^{\overline{\phi}}[\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi)]\mathcal{F}(\phi)\,\mathrm{d}\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mmultiscripts><mo>∫</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mprescripts></mprescripts><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mstyle><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi),R(\phi))}\int_{\underline{\phi}}^{\overline{\phi}}[\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi)-I)+1)-R(\phi)]\mathcal{F}(\phi)\,\mathrm{d}\phi</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.440em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\underline{\phi}R(\underline{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\underline{\phi})\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\phi R(\phi)-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq f\phi R(\hat{\phi})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\hat{\phi}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E9Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq I,\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi)\geq I,\&gt;\phi,\hat{\phi}\in[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The solution to Problem 2 mainly proceeds in two stages. The first is to calculate <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> using LMM agents, and the second is to derive the optimal reward <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\star}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\star}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> based on the calculated <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring that the utility of the platform can be maximized under the IR and IC constraints. Since contracts can be more easily broadcast in a limited number of forms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>)</cite>, we quantize the continuous distribution of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> with a discrete one consisting of a finite set of points. Specifically, we first quantize the type set <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\underline{\phi},\overline{\phi}]</annotation></semantics></math> using a quantization factor <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby reducing it to a discrete set of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> types, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi=\{\phi_{1},\phi_{2},\ldots,\phi_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi=\{\phi_{1},\phi_{2},\ldots,\phi_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quantization process is uniform with equidistant values <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>)</cite>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}=\underline{\phi}+\frac{k-1}{K}(\overline{\phi}-\underline{\phi}),\&gt;k\in[1,\ldots,K]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}=\underline{\phi}+\frac{k-1}{K}(\overline{\phi}-\underline{\phi}),\&gt;k\in[1,\ldots,K]</annotation></semantics></math>, sorted as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{1}&lt;\phi_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;\phi_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{1}&lt;\phi_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;\phi_{K}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, the probability of users belonging to type <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is formulated by applying the forward difference method, which can be expressed as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasan and Bhargava, 2013</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(10)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{k}=\mathcal{P}(\phi_{k}\leq\phi\leq\phi_{k+1})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{k+1})-\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{k}),\&gt;1\leq k&lt;K,\\
\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\overline{\phi})-\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{K}),\&gt;\quad k=K,\\
\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.387em">,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{k}=\mathcal{P}(\phi_{k}\leq\phi\leq\phi_{k+1})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{k+1})-\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{k}),\&gt;1\leq k&lt;K,\\
\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\overline{\phi})-\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\phi_{K}),\&gt;\quad k=K,\\
\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where we have <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{K}\delta_{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{K}\delta_{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{F}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cumulative distribution function of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math>, following the beta distribution. Therefore, Problem 2 can be reformulated as</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E11X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(11)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 3:}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">Problem 3:</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\textbf{Problem 3:}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k}),R(\phi_{k}))}\Bigg[\mathbb{E}(\mathcal{U}_{P})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\delta_{k}(\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})-I)+1)-R(\phi_{k}))\Bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></msup><mi>δ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{(\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k}),R(\phi_{k}))}\Bigg[\mathbb{E}(\mathcal{U}_{P})=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\delta_{k}(\eta\ln((\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})-I)+1)-R(\phi_{k}))\Bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\phi_{1}R(\phi_{1})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{1})\geq 0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mo lspace="0.440em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\&gt;f\phi_{1}R(\phi_{1})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{1})\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f\phi_{k}R(\phi_{k})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})\geq f\phi_{k}R(\phi_{j})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{j})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f\phi_{k}R(\phi_{k})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})\geq f\phi_{k}R(\phi_{j})-\kappa\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{j}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{1}&lt;\phi_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;\phi_{K}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{1}&lt;\phi_{2}&lt;\cdots&lt;\phi_{K},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E11Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})\geq I,\&gt;k,j\in[1,K]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{k})\geq I,\&gt;k,j\in[1,K].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Although the number of IR constraints is reduced, it is still difficult to use traditional optimization approaches to solve Problem 3 under the existing IC constraints. Moreover, in practice, environment parameters such as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> may change over time, necessitating the redesign of traditional approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Hence, traditional optimization approaches may not provide an effective solution to Problem 3. DRL algorithms have been widely applied to network optimization problems, as they can learn policies in dynamic environments with incomplete information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and achieve near-optimal solutions in scenarios where traditional methods often struggle.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04765v1/MoE-PPO.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/268;" width="452" height="268" alt="MoE-based PPO for optimal contract design.">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3. </span>The diagram of the MoE-based PPO algorithm for optimal contract design. LMM agents are first enhanced through prompt engineering (<span id="S3.F3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1</span>), after which they evaluate the quality of the input UGC (<span id="S3.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2</span>). In <span id="S3.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Steps 3 to 8</span>, the training process of the MoE-based PPO algorithm is presented.<span id="acmlabel3" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">MoE-based PPO for optimal contract design.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the improved MoE-based PPO algorithm for optimal contract design. We first model Problem 3 as a Markov Decision Process (MDP). We then present the architecture of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm, as shown in Fig. <a href="#acmlabel3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. It is worth noting that the MoE-based PPO algorithm is implemented on the reward platform to optimize contract design.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>MDP Formulation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MDP framework, we formulate Problem 3 through the components <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\gamma\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},\mathcal{R},\gamma\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> is the state space consisting of state vectors <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> is the action space consisting of action vectors <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward function, and <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the discount factor governing the trade-off between present and future returns. We detail these components as follows:</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1. </span>State vector</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The derivation of optimal contracts begins with LMM agents quantifying <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by a DRL agent that learns the optimal reward <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\star}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\star}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> based on the estimated <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we incorporate <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> into the state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(12)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\triangleq\{\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{1}),\ldots,\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{K}),I,\kappa,K,\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{K},\phi_{1},\ldots,\phi_{K}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\triangleq\{\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{1}),\ldots,\mathcal{Q}(\phi_{K}),I,\kappa,K,\delta_{1},\ldots,\delta_{K},\phi_{1},\ldots,\phi_{K}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the total dimensionality of the state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3K+3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3K+3)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2. </span>Action vector</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The objective of the DRL agent is to determine the optimal reward <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\star}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\star}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math> conditioned on the state <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐬</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, the action of the DRL agent <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(13)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}\triangleq\{R(\phi_{1}),\ldots,R(\phi_{K})\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>≜</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}\triangleq\{R(\phi_{1}),\ldots,R(\phi_{K})\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where the total dimensionality of the action <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3. </span>Reward function</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The reward function plays a significant role in guiding the DRL agent to maximize the expected utility of the reward platform while satisfying the constraints in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>). Thus, the reward function <math id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> for the DRL agent is defined as</p>
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(14)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}=r(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\mathbb{E}(\mathcal{U}_{P}),\&gt;\text{if constraints in (\ref{problem3}) are satisfied},\\
0,\&gt;\quad\quad\&gt;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>P</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>if constraints in (</mtext><mtext><a href="#S3.E11" title="In 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a></mtext><mtext>) are satisfied</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="2.607em">,</mo><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}=r(\mathbf{s},\mathbf{a})=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\mathbb{E}(\mathcal{U}_{P}),\&gt;\text{if constraints in (\ref{problem3}) are satisfied},\\
0,\&gt;\quad\quad\&gt;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>MoE-based PPO Architecture</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PPO is one of the most representative DRL algorithms, which has been widely applied across various domains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>. Notably, PPO lays the foundation for large language model alignment owing to its stable training dynamics and strong adaptability in complex environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>. However, when handling complex tasks, PPO requires relatively high computational demands, and limited resources can adversely affect its convergence speed. MoE is a machine learning architecture composed of a gating network and multiple sub-networks, referred to as <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">experts</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Each expert is trained to specialize in a subset of the input data, while the gating network selectively activates only a subset of experts for each input, thereby enabling high model capacity with efficient computation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In particular, MoE layers can be broadly categorized into two types: dense MoE and sparse MoE. A dense MoE layer activates all experts simultaneously, whereas a sparse MoE layer activates only a selected subset of experts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, MoE has been integrated into PPO to enhance the capacity of the policy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>. For instance, a dense MoE-based PPO algorithm was proposed to solve the sum-rate maximization problem in heterogeneous satellite networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Motivated by this, we propose an improved MoE-based PPO algorithm, which adopts an actor-critic framework, to design optimal contracts. Specifically, the actor network, parameterized by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math>, is augmented with an ensemble of experts to facilitate decision-making, while the critic network, parameterized by <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>, assesses the state-value function <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math> to guide policy improvement.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1. </span>MoE-based actor network</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider an MoE-based actor network consisting of <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> expert networks, denoted as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{1}},\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{2}},\ldots,\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{1}},\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{2}},\ldots,\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each expert is a perception with unique parameters <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. To select the most relevant experts based on the current state feature <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, the central gating network first generates unnormalized expert weights <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>M</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})\in\mathbb{R}^{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, as given by</p>
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(15)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{g}\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}_{g}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{W}_{g}\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{b}_{g},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{W}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐖</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{W}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learnable weight matrix, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐛</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> is a bias vector. Then, these expert weights <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>𝝋</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi}}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> are normalized through the softmax function to produce the expert selection probabilities, expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(16)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}=\frac{\exp(g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}(\mathbf{x}))}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}\exp(g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},j}(\mathbf{x}))},\&gt;i=1,2,\ldots,M." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}=\frac{\exp(g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}(\mathbf{x}))}{\sum_{j=1}^{M}\exp(g_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},j}(\mathbf{x}))},\&gt;i=1,2,\ldots,M.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the sparse setting, instead of activating all experts, the gating network selects only the top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> experts with the highest probabilities, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, the weights corresponding to the top-<math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> experts are retained and re-normalized, while those of the remaining experts are set to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(17)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\frac{p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}}{\sum_{j\in\text{top-}m}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},j}},\&gt;i\in\text{top-}m,\\
0,\&gt;\quad\quad\&gt;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{array}\right." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mtext>top-</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="2.607em">,</mo><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mphantom></mphantom></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}=\left\{\begin{array}[]{l}\frac{p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}}{\sum_{j\in\text{top-}m}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},j}},\&gt;i\in\text{top-}m,\\
0,\&gt;\quad\quad\&gt;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{array}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Based on (<a href="#S4.E17" title="In 4.2.1. MoE-based actor network ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>), the mean of the MoE-based policy is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(18)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})=\sum_{i=1}^{M}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{i}}(\mathbf{x})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})=\sum_{i=1}^{M}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\mathcal{E}_{\boldsymbol{\psi}_{i}}(\mathbf{x}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">To enable more stable training, we define the standard deviation as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})=\exp(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\sigma})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})=\exp(\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\sigma})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbf{a}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝝎</mi><mi>σ</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\omega}_{\sigma}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathbf{a}|}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent learnable parameters. Therefore, the policy network of the proposed MoE-based PPO framework is given by</p>
<table id="S4.E19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(19)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{a}\mid\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s}),\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s}))," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}|\mathbf{s})=\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{a}\mid\mu_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s}),\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is a diagonal covariance matrix, expressed as <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{diag}\big(\sigma^{2}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>diag</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{diag}\big(\sigma^{2}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{s})\big)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2. </span>Objective function of MoE-based PPO</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">According to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the objective function of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm preserves the core principles of PPO, which is formulated as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S4.E20X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_left">(20)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}\Bigg[\mathcal{L}_{A}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})\bigg]\Bigg]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}\Bigg[\mathcal{L}_{A}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})\bigg]\Bigg]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.E20Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\mathbb{E}_{t}\big[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})\|\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t}))\big]\leq\varepsilon," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\&gt;\mathbb{E}_{t}\big[D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})\|\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t}))\big]\leq\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kullback-Leibler (KL) divergence function, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive hyperparameter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In practice, the expectation <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is approximated by an empirical average <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{E}}_{t}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{E}}_{t}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> over a batch of sampled trajectories. <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the variance-reduced advantage function, which can be estimated by using the Generalized Advantage Estimation (GAE) based on the state-value function <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐬</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be formulated as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(21)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})=\sum_{\ell=0}^{T-t-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{\ell}\Delta_{t+\ell},\&gt;\Delta_{t}=r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})+\gamma V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t+1})-V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})=\sum_{\ell=0}^{T-t-1}(\gamma\lambda)^{\ell}\Delta_{t+\ell},\&gt;\Delta_{t}=r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})+\gamma V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t+1})-V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum step in an episode, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is a balance factor that trades off bias and variance in advantage estimation. In particular, when <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, GAE reduces to the Monte Carlo estimation of the advantage function, expressed as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(22)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})=\gamma^{T-t}V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{T})-V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})+\sum_{\ell=t}^{T-1}\gamma^{\ell-t}r(\mathbf{s}_{\ell},\mathbf{a}_{\ell})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})=\gamma^{T-t}V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{T})-V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})+\sum_{\ell=t}^{T-1}\gamma^{\ell-t}r(\mathbf{s}_{\ell},\mathbf{a}_{\ell}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate computation, the objective function in (<a href="#S4.E20" title="In 4.2.2. Objective function of MoE-based PPO ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a>) is directly constrained to ensure that the update from <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math> is not excessively large, rather than considering the KL divergence constraint <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Thus, the objective function <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{A}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{A}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})</annotation></semantics></math> can be approximated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(23)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{CLIP}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\min\bigg(\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t}),\mathrm{clip}\bigg(\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\bigg)A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})\bigg)\bigg]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>CLIP</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{CLIP}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\min\bigg(\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t}),\mathrm{clip}\bigg(\frac{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})}{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\mathbf{a}_{t}|\mathbf{s}_{t})},1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon\bigg)A^{\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}}(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})\bigg)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{clip}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>clip</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{clip}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the clip function that restricts the probability ratio to the range <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1-\epsilon,1+\epsilon]</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> being a predefined hyperparameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3. </span>Update mechanisms of policy and critic</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our MoE-based PPO implementation, the parameters of both the actor and critic networks are optimized by minimizing their respective loss functions. Specifically, the loss function of the critic network is given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(24)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{C}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c})=\mathbb{E}_{t}[\|V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})-V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})\|^{2}]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>tar</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{C}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c})=\mathbb{E}_{t}[\|V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})-V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})\|^{2}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>tar</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the target return estimated from the trajectory, as given by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite></p>
<table id="S4.E25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(25)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})=r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})+\cdots+\gamma^{\ell+1}V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t+\ell+1})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>tar</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐚</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>+</mo><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\mathrm{tar}}(\mathbf{s}_{t})=r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t})+\cdots+\gamma^{\ell+1}V_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}}(\mathbf{s}_{t+\ell+1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, the parameters of the critic network are updated through mini-batch Stochastic Gradient Descent (SGD) using experiences sampled from the environment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(26)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}-\tau_{c}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{t=1}^{B}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}\mathcal{L}_{C}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{c}-\tau_{c}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{t=1}^{B}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}}\mathcal{L}_{C}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{c}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{c}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learning rate of the critic network, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the mini-batch size.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To encourage balanced utilization of experts, we incorporate an MoE-specific auxiliary loss function into the total loss function of the actor network, which is formulated as</p>
<table id="S4.E27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(27)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MoE}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=-\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^{M}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\log p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\bigg]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>𝝋</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MoE}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=-\mathbb{E}_{t}\bigg[\sum_{i=1}^{M}p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\log p_{\boldsymbol{\varphi},i}^{\prime}\bigg].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the total function of the actor network is expressed as</p>
<table id="S4.E28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(28)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E28.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{Total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{CLIP}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})+\omega_{\mathrm{MoE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MoE}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})-\omega_{\mathrm{Ent}}\mathbb{E}_{t}\big[\mathcal{H}\big[\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}_{t})\big]\big]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>Total</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>CLIP</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Ent</mi></msub><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{Total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})=\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{CLIP}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})+\omega_{\mathrm{MoE}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{MoE}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})-\omega_{\mathrm{Ent}}\mathbb{E}_{t}\big[\mathcal{H}\big[\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}_{t})\big]\big],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{H}\big[\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}_{t})\big]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}\big[\pi_{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}}(\cdot|\mathbf{s}_{t})\big]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the entropy of the action probability distribution, encouraging exploration by discouraging overly deterministic policies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mathrm{MoE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mathrm{MoE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{\mathrm{Ent}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>Ent</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{\mathrm{Ent}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the weight coefficients of the MoE auxiliary loss and the entropy, respectively. Thus, the parameters of the actor network are updated by the following function:</p>
<table id="S4.E29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(29)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}-\tau_{a}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{t=1}^{B}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{Total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi><mi>old</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>B</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub></msub><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>Total</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}\leftarrow\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\mathrm{old}}_{a}-\tau_{a}\frac{1}{B}\sum_{t=1}^{B}\nabla_{\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}}\mathcal{L}_{A}^{\mathrm{Total}}(\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{a}\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{a}\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the learning rate of the actor network.</p>
</div>
