              <span id="alg1.6.11.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Store record </span><math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t},\mathbf{s}_{t+1},r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐬</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐚</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐬</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐚</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t},\mathbf{s}_{t+1},r(\mathbf{s}_{t},\mathbf{a}_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.11.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> into the relay buffer </span><math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.11.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.12" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.12.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">12</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">end for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.13" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.13.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">13</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">### Parameter updates ###</span><span id="alg1.6.13.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.14" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.14.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.14.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">14</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Sample a random mini-batch of transitions with size </span><math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.800em">B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.14.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> from </span><math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.14.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.15" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.15.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">15</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.15.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Update the critic parameters </span><math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝜽</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{c}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.15.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> through SGD by using (</span><a href="#S4.E26" title="In 4.2.3. Update mechanisms of policy and critic ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a><span id="alg1.6.15.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">).
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.16" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.16.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">16</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.16.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Update the policy parameters </span><math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝜽</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">a</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{a}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.16.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> through SGD by using (</span><a href="#S4.E29" title="In 4.2.3. Update mechanisms of policy and critic ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">29</span></a><span id="alg1.6.16.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">).
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.17" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="alg1.6.17.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">  </span>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.17.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">17</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.17.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Update the current state </span><math id="alg1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{t}\leftarrow\mathbf{s}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐬</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝐬</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{t}\leftarrow\mathbf{s}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.17.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.18" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.18.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">18</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.18.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">end for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.19" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.19.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">19</span></span>
              <span id="alg1.6.19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:80%;">Output:</span><span id="alg1.6.19.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;"> The optimal reward </span><math id="alg1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{\star}(\phi)=\{R^{\star}(\phi_{1}),\ldots,R^{\star}(\phi_{K})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">K</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{\star}(\phi)=\{R^{\star}(\phi_{1}),\ldots,R^{\star}(\phi_{K})\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.6.19.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.6.20" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.6.20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The pseudocode of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm is presented in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In 4.2.3. Update mechanisms of policy and critic ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The computational complexity of Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In 4.2.3. Update mechanisms of policy and critic ‣ 4.2. MoE-based PPO Architecture ‣ 4. Improved MoE-based PPO for Optimal Contract Design ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> consists of two components: <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{MoE}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>MoE</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{MoE}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{Critic}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>Critic</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{Critic}}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the one hand, the computational complexity of the MoE-based actor network mainly comes from the gating network and the experts, which is given by <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(BM|\mathbf{x}|)+\mathcal{O}(Bm|\mathbf{x}||\mathbf{a}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(BM|\mathbf{x}|)+\mathcal{O}(Bm|\mathbf{x}||\mathbf{a}|)</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{x}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{x}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the state feature dimensionality, and <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathbf{a}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathbf{a}|</annotation></semantics></math> is the action dimensionality. On the other hand, the computational complexity of the critic network is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(Bh^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(Bh^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, where <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is the hidden size of the critic network. Therefore, the total computational complexity of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm is <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(B(M|\mathbf{x}|+m|\mathbf{x}||\mathbf{a}|+h^{2}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐚</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(B(M|\mathbf{x}|+m|\mathbf{x}||\mathbf{a}|+h^{2}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Simulation Results</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first provide the details of the experimental setup. We then evaluate the performance of the proposed MoE-based PPO in the context of optimal contract design. Finally, we implement the designed contract within an Ethereum smart contract framework.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.1. </span>Experimental platform</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments to evaluate the performance of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm for optimal contract design, implemented in PyTorch with CUDA 12.0 and executed on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3080 Laptop GPU. Furthermore, we deploy the designed contract within Remix IDE using Solidity 0.8.17. Remix IDE is an Ethereum-based smart contract development platform maintained by the Ethereum community, which provides an integrated environment for writing, compiling, debugging, and deploying smart contracts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.2. </span>Environment details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For clarity, we consider user reputation with two types: low and high <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, i.e., <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Regarding the environment parameters, the threshold of UGC quality <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, the unit cost of generating content <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly sampled from the interval <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,3)</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>, the maximum user reputation value <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> and the minimum user reputation value <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\phi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\phi}</annotation></semantics></math> are randomly sampled within <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[5,10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[5,10)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[10,15)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[10,15)</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, and the shape parameters of the beta distribution <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta</annotation></semantics></math> are both randomly sampled within <math id="S5.SS1.SSS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[1,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[1,2]</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deshprabhu, 2024</a>)</cite>, where the probability functions for two user types are presented in Fig. <a href="#acmlabel4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1.3. Algorithm design ‣ 5.1. Experimental Setup ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Moreover, to enhance training efficiency and reduce computational costs, we create an LMM simulator to generate evaluation results of UGC. During the test stage, the LMM simulator can be replaced with the real LMM agent (e.g., the GPT-5 agent) through the API.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.3. </span>Algorithm design</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the actor network structure, both the gating network and the expert networks are implemented as linear layers in the context of our contract design, thereby reducing the computational complexity of the actor network. To effectively evaluate the policy, the critic network is designed with two fully-connected layers, each of size <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>256</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">256</annotation></semantics></math>, followed by <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Tanh}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Tanh</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Tanh}</annotation></semantics></math> activation functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. The parameters of both the actor and critic networks are updated using the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, with a weight decay rate of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.0001</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.0001</annotation></semantics></math> applied to regularize the model weights <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>. For other hyperparameter settings, the batch size is set to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, the discount factor <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, the value function coefficient to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, the clipping parameter <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>, the maximum norm for gradient clipping to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024c</a>)</cite>, and the balance factor <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.SS1.SSS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024d</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04765v1/probability.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:452/106;" width="452" height="106" aria-label="Type probability function."><p>Type probability function.</p></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>The probability functions for two user types with the shape parameter <math id="S5.F4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> as the independent variable. We consider user reputation with two types: low and high <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhan et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and set the value of <math id="S5.F4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S5.F4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that, when <math id="S5.F4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed, the probability function with the shape parameter <math id="S5.F4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> as the independent variable is symmetrical to the current probability function.<span id="acmlabel4" class="ltx_acm_description ltx_centering ltx_nodisplay">Type probability function.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.1.4. </span>Benchmark comparisons</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm with three representative on-policy DRL algorithms, including PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulman et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, transformer-based PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and tiny PPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kang et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>, as well as two widely used off-policy DRL algorithms, including SAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haarnoja et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and the GDM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We evaluate all algorithms in a discrete action space to ensure fair comparisons. Moreover, we compare the proposed scheme under asymmetric information with three schemes:</p>
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contract scheme under complete information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hou et al., 2018</a>)</cite>:</span> The reward platform has access to user reputation information (i.e., user types) without considering the IC constraints (<a href="#S3.E4" title="In Definition 0. ‣ 3.2.3. LMM-based contract formulation ‣ 3.2. LMM-based Contract-Theoretic Model ‣ 3. Problem Formulation ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Random scheme under asymmetric information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>:</span> The reward platform designs contracts randomly, without regard to user types.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Average scheme under asymmetric information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>:</span> The reward platform designs identical contracts for all users, regardless of user types.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#acmlabel5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.2. Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we conduct the performance evaluation of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm in optimal contract design. From Fig. <a href="#S5.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 5.2. Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, we observe that our contract scheme under asymmetric information outperforms the baseline schemes, namely the random and average schemes under asymmetric information. In addition, the contract scheme under complete information achieves significantly higher test rewards than the proposed contract scheme. The reason is that the reward platform has access to user type information and thus provides the most suitable contract item for each user <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. However, in practice, it is difficult for the reward platform to precisely identify user type information, and a complete information environment is unrealistic. Therefore, the contract scheme under complete information can be seen as an ideal benchmark. Figures <a href="#S5.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 5.2. Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ 5.2. Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a> show performance comparisons of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm with three on-policy DRL algorithms and two off-policy DRL algorithms in the context of contract design. To present the comparison results more clearly, we summarize the performance achieved by the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm and these DRL benchmarks in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.2. Performance Evaluation of MoE-based PPO ‣ 5. Simulation Results ‣ LMM-Incentive: Large Multimodal Model-based Incentive Design for User-Generated Content in Web 3.0" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, in terms of test rewards, train rewards, final rewards, total time, and convergence time. We see that the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm achieves the highest test, train, and final rewards among all benchmarks. Moreover, it converges faster than PPO. These results are attributed to the collaborative ability of experts, demonstrating the superior characteristics of the proposed MoE-based PPO algorithm, including <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high performance</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">fast convergence</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure" aria-describedby="acmlabel5">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
